# Shared patterns

These behaviours repeat almost identically across Dsc, Nws, Nwl, Cln (and partly Rwd, Rfl, Msg). Each is built
ONCE as a shared abstraction (hook + UI + types) and configured per service. Never re-implement one inside a
service. If a service needs a variation, add an option to the shared version.

## 1. Approval workflow

- Provider (ASR) or user submits → admin (Adm) approves or rejects. Rejection requires a reason picked from the
  service's managed reason list, plus optional free text.
- Edits to approved/published items create a pending change; admin approves/rejects per field (Dsc-2 pattern)
  with an optional replacement value that is sent back to the counterpart for approval.
- The counterpart actor is notified on submit, approve, reject (texts from the SRS).
- Status transitions are defined in one state map per entity in its `types` module; the UI derives available
  actions from `status × role`, never from ad hoc conditions.

## 2. Soft delete and restore

- Delete = soft delete. The item stays listed with a Restore action until the retention period passes, then the
  backend deletes it physically. The period is backend configuration — never hardcode it in the frontend.
- Some deletes are requests (provider requests delete/restore → admin decides). Use the shared request flow.
- Deleting something that is in use (reason, category, voucher) shows the SRS in-use error path.

## 3. Archive / unarchive

Separate from delete. Archived items leave default lists and appear in an "archived" view/tab.

## 4. Two-level categories (main / sub)

- One shared category manager: list, search, add main, add sub, edit, soft delete, restore.
- Errors: duplicate main name, duplicate sub name (within its parent), delete main that has subs.
- Deleted categories are hidden from pickers but kept on existing records.

## 5. Managed reason lists

- Rejection / cancellation / suspension reasons: list, search, add, edit, soft delete, restore.
- Error on deleting a reason already used. Unused reasons may be auto soft-deleted by the system (`Sys` paths).

## 6. Notifications

- Emitted by the backend; the frontend only renders them. Never construct notification side effects client-side.

## 7. Audit log

- Privileged and moderation actions are logged by the backend (actor id, entity id, action, timestamp, reason).
  The frontend never writes audit records; it passes the reason and shows history screens.

## 8. Reports, statistics, charts

- Every service has a reports area: history tables (shared list shell), stat cards, charts. Use the shared
  report layout, stat card and chart wrapper; lazy-load charts with `next/dynamic`.
- Reports obey the same role scoping as the screens they summarise.

## 9. Uploads

- One shared uploader with per-field config (formats, max size, dimensions, required). SRS limits in use today:
  featured image ≤ 1 MB (jpg, png, webp, avif); list image ≤ 500 KB; newsletter file PDF ≤ 3 MB;
  discount-code import xlsx with one column. Client checks are UX; the server re-validates.
- Wrong format and wrong size each map to their own SRS E-path message.

## 10. Guest and restricted-actor access

- Guest trying an authenticated action → the SRS guest-access error path (prompt to log in / register).
- Legal-entity providers (سرویس‌دهنده حقوقی) are blocked from some user actions (e.g. referral links, some
  interactive news actions) with the SRS message. Model this as a capability, not a string check.

## 11. Input conventions from the SRS

- Name fields: Save/Confirm disabled until something changed and the minimum length (usually 3) is met.
- Search fields: max 50 characters.
- Date/time fields: if time is omitted, the system ignores time (date-only semantics).
